  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 355 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nThe upper level low will continue to dig southeast across the \nsouthwestern U.S. on Wednesday with a warmer airmass remaining \nin place across the area. Above normal temperatures will again \noccur on Wednesday as highs warm into the middle to upper 80s \nand a few locations could approach the 90 degree mark. Gusty \nsouth to southwesterly winds will accompany the warmer air mass \nas gusts up to around 30 mph occurs at times on Wednesday. \n\nThe upper level low will remain well to the southwest of the \narea on Wednesday resulting in much of the day remaining dry. \nThe long wave trough will remain west of the area with the \nwestern portions of the area on the eastern edge of the trough.\nTherefore, energy will lift more to the north from the low and \nremain west of the area through much of the day on Wednesday. \nWednesday night into Thursday the trough will start to slowly \nmove to the east. Moisture advection will occur ahead of the \nsystem and the slow movement will allow a longer return period. \nThe remnants of Hurricane Polo will also be absorbed into the \nupper level low before moving into the region also increasing \nmoisture with the system.\n\nBands of showers, with some embedded thunderstorms will develop\nahead of the low and track to the north/northeast. As the \ntrough slowly moves east these bands could start to move into \nthe western portions of the area Wednesday evening, but more \nlikely Wednesday night into Thursday morning. The bands will \ncontinue to develop and track to the north/northeast on Thursday\nand spread east through the day. The upper level low will start\nslowly move east therefore the bands may not reach the eastern \nportions of the area until later in the day. The slow movement \nof the trough will lead to the potential for training of these \nbands as the keep develop and tracking over the same locations. \nThe increased moisture and the training potential will lead to \nthe risk for some heavy rainfall across portions of the the \narea. The WPC has locations along and west of I-49 in a Slight \nRisk for Excessive Rain for Wednesday night and a Slight Risk \nfor much of the area on Thursday/Thursday night as the system \nslowly moves to the east.\n\nHighs will only warm into the 70s on Thursday where the rain\nmoves into the area, but with the slow movement of the system\nthe rain may not move into the eastern portions of the area\nuntil later in the day allowing highs to potentially warm into\nthe lower to middle 80s where the rain holds off until longer\ninto the day. \n\nAnother upper level trough will move east across the northern \nPlains late week and will push a front to the south into the \narea Friday/Friday night. The upper level low to the southwest\nshould start to move east through towards the area Friday, and \nwill likely open up into a trough as the long wave trough \nbecome less amplified. The front and trough will result in \nadditional periods of rain occurring across the area on \nFriday/Friday night. The ensemble model members differ on the \ntiming of the front as some move it south of the area Friday \nnight, with others moving the front south of the area on \nSaturday, and a few members (therefore lower potential at time) \nshowing the front stalling over the area through much of the \nweekend. Rain chances will occur across the area until the front\nmoves south of the area. The front will bring cooler conditions\nto the area with highs in the upper 60s into the middle 70s \nexpected this weekend into early next week.",
